# Fathom — Debug and automation API

Fathom ships a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven and
inspected from code, without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time. It is
what you use to iterate on the sensing and the predators, reproduce a specific hunt,
script a scenario, and capture clean screenshots of an exact
game state. This file defines that surface. Implement all of it, on the same footing
as the game itself.

Nothing here changes how a person plays. The debug API is inert during normal play,
doing nothing until something calls it, and the debug overlay is off until toggled.

## A deterministic core

The whole surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable, which
the fixed-step core in `specs/movement.md` already requires: a fixed timestep,
integrated in whole steps, decoupled from rendering. Two more properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances by stepping the simulation and must not
  depend on a canvas, on `requestAnimationFrame`, or on wall-clock time to make
  progress. The dependency runs one way: the simulation never reads from, waits
  on, or is driven by the renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses (predator and drifter wander, the
  bonus-drifter spawn cadence) runs off a seedable generator, so reseeding and
  replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of API calls and steps, the game reaches
the same state every time.

## The manual clock

The simulation advances on a fixed timestep of `120 Hz` (`specs/movement.md`), so one
simulation tick is exactly `1/120 s`. During normal play the animation-frame loop
drives those ticks from the wall clock; the debug API can drive them directly.

The game holds an `autoStep` flag, on by default (normal human play). The
animation-frame loop advances the simulation only when `autoStep` is on. When it is
off, the game still renders every frame but does not advance the simulation on its
own.

- `reset()` and `step(ticks)` turn `autoStep` off, beginning a driver-clocked
  session. While it is off, `step(ticks)` is the only thing that advances the
  simulation, so a stepped scenario is exact and reproducible regardless of machine
  load: no stray wall-clock frames can slip into a measurement window.
- `setAutoStep(true)` lets the game advance itself in real time again (for watching
  or recording a motion clip); `setAutoStep(false)` returns to manual stepping.
  `reset()` re-arms manual stepping.
- Injecting input (`keyDown`, `keyUp`, `press`) and the other control operations do
  not change `autoStep`.

So a caller takes exact measurements in the default manual mode (each `step(n)`
advances the simulation by exactly `n` ticks), and switches to `setAutoStep(true)`
only to let a live clip play out in real time.

## The `window.__fathom` object

Expose the API as a single object on the global `window.__fathom`, installed once the
game is running. It carries a `version` number (use `1`) and the operations below.
Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans so a caller can read them directly;
positions and speeds are in the logical-pixel space of `specs/overview.md`, and tile
coordinates are the `(column, row)` of the grid defined there.

### Core operations

- `reset(options)` returns the game to its initial title state. `options` is
  optional, and `options.seed` (a number) seeds all of the game's randomness so a
  scenario replays identically. `reset` re-arms manual stepping (see The manual
  clock).
- `step(ticks)` advances the simulation by exactly `ticks` fixed steps immediately,
  running the fixed-timestep update internally rather than waiting for real frames.
  The unit is whole simulation ticks, not seconds: the timestep is `120 Hz`, so one
  tick is `1/120 s`, `step(1)` runs a single step and `step(120)` advances one second
  of game time. Nothing is rounded or approximated — the number of steps asked for is
  the number of steps run. `ticks` must be a non-negative integer; `step(0)` is legal
  and does nothing, while a fractional or negative value is invalid and the call fails
  loudly rather than guessing what was meant. This runs the real systems forward from
  a set-up state to see where they land. Stepping advances the live dive (play or its
  countdown) and has no effect on a menu screen. Calling `step` (or `reset`) also
  switches the game to manual stepping, so successive steps advance the simulation by
  exactly the number of ticks asked for, with no stray wall-clock frames creeping in
  between calls.
- `setAutoStep(enabled)` turns automatic (wall-clock) stepping on or off (see The
  manual clock).
- `snapshot()` returns a plain, JSON-serializable object describing the current game
  state (see [Snapshot shape](#snapshot-shape)). It is a pure read and never changes
  anything.

### Control operations

These set up a specific situation. Each one routes through the same systems normal
play uses, arranging the world rather than faking an outcome: they only establish a
precondition, and what a caller then observes is produced by stepping the real
simulation, never announced by the op itself.

- `startDive()` begins a dive, exactly as choosing `DIVE` from the title menu would.
  The dive opens on the pre-start dive countdown.
- `beginPlay()` ends the dive countdown now, entering live play immediately instead
  of waiting it out.
- `setDepth(d)` sets the current depth to the integer `d` (with `1` the first maze).
  The real depth-scaling (`specs/progression.md`) then governs the derived values: the
  predator roster (how many of each hunter) and the sonar range recompute from `d`, so
  a caller sets the depth and reads the scaled results back from `snapshot()`.
- `setForager(state)` places the forager. `state` may set `tx` and `ty` (a tile the
  forager is moved to, snapped to that tile's center through the movement system) and
  `dir` (its facing, one of `"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"`, `"right"`). The tile must be
  an open corridor tile. The forager is placed at rest there (as if no movement key
  is held); injected input then moves it through the game's normal movement code.
- `setBrightness(g)` sets the forager's brightness `G` to `g` in `[0, 1]`, as a
  precondition. The real formulas recompute from it: the light radius `V`{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}, the vision-circle radius `R`,{{/if}} and the Lanternjaw's and Flarefish's light detection ranges all follow `g` per `specs/gameplay.md` and `specs/predators.md`. Eating plankton is the normal way `G` rises; this op sets it directly rather than raising it by grazing a corridor first.
- `setPredator(kind, state)` places a predator. `kind` is `"lanternjaw"`,
  `"gloamfin"`, or `"flarefish"`. `state` may set `tx` and `ty` (a corridor tile it
  is moved to), `dir` (its facing), and `mode`, one of `"den"` (returned to the den),
  `"wander"` (patrolling, not fixed on you), or `"chase"` (fixed on the forager's
  current tile and pursuing). After posing it, the predator's own AI runs when the
  simulation is stepped: it senses, acquires, chases, and searches through the real
  code, so a scenario poses the setup and reads what the AI then does.
  - `"den"` HOLDS it there: the staggered release schedule (`specs/predators.md`) is
    suspended for a predator posed this way, and it stays in the den chamber until a
    later `setPredator` poses it out, however long the scenario runs. This is what
    lets a caller quiet the board and read one predator's behavior undisturbed. A
    scenario that needs a predator loose poses it to `"wander"` or `"chase"`
    directly.
- `spawnDrifter(state)` adds a bonus drifter. `state` may set `tx` and `ty` (a
  corridor tile it appears on); with no tile it spawns at the den gate as in normal
  play. It then wanders through the real drifter code.
- `setCreatureAI(enabled)` turns the creatures' own minds on or off, where a creature
  is every predator and every bonus drifter. It is on by default, and `reset` restores
  it.

  With it off, each creature holds exactly where it stands, keeping the tile, facing,
  and state it was posed with: it senses nothing, decides nothing, and does not move,
  however long the scenario runs. Nothing else changes — the forager still swims under
  its own controls, its brightness and light still follow what it eats, cooldowns still
  run down, sonar wavefronts and ink clouds still travel and expire, plankton and
  drifters are still eaten and still score, and contact with a predator still costs a
  life. Only the creatures' own initiative is suspended.

  This is what makes a scenario about *interacting with* a creature reproducible. A
  wandering predator or drifter goes where its own mind takes it, so a caller that poses
  one and then swims at it is betting on where it happens to have drifted by the time
  the forager arrives — a bet that turns on the layout of a maze the build invented, and
  one that is lost outright when the creature wanders down a side corridor. Turning the
  minds off puts the creature where the caller put it and leaves it there, so what the
  scenario observes is the interaction it set out to observe.
- `poseLastPlankton()` removes every plankton except a single one placed on an open
  tile adjacent to the forager, so that the forager eating that one plankton clears
  the maze and descends through the real clear-and-descend path. It arranges the
  precondition; the real eat is what triggers the descent.
- `clearCooldowns()` makes the sonar pulse and ink immediately ready, so either
  one can fire at once rather than waiting out its cooldown.

A typical check calls `reset({ seed })`, `startDive()`, `beginPlay()`, then poses the
forager and a predator with `setForager` and `setPredator`, `step()`s a handful of
ticks to run the real sensing and movement, and reads the result from `snapshot()`.

### Input operations

The control operations above pose the world directly. The API can also inject
keyboard input, so a caller can drive the game the way a player does: navigate the
menus, start a dive, move the forager by holding a direction, ping with sonar, drop
ink, pause, and toggle mute. Injected input flows through the same handling the real
keyboard feeds, exercising the actual key bindings from `specs/movement.md` rather
than a parallel path. A held movement key moves the forager through the game's normal
play code, so this is how a caller confirms the controls themselves work.

- `keyDown(code)` presses a key down. `code` is a standard `KeyboardEvent.code` (for
  example `"ArrowUp"`, `"ArrowDown"`, `"ArrowLeft"`, `"ArrowRight"`, `"KeyW"`,
  `"KeyA"`, `"KeyS"`, `"KeyD"`, `"Space"`, `"ShiftLeft"`, `"Enter"`, `"Escape"`,
  `"KeyP"`, `"KeyM"`). The key becomes held, so a movement key drives the forager
  while it is held and the simulation is stepped, and any one-shot action the key
  triggers on the current screen (a menu move, a confirm, a sonar ping, an ink drop,
  a pause, a mute toggle) is applied immediately.
- `keyUp(code)` releases a previously pressed key, ending its held state.
- `press(code)` is a convenience tap, a `keyDown` immediately followed by `keyUp`.
  This is the usual way to trigger a one-shot action (moving a menu selection,
  confirming it, pinging, inking, pausing, muting) without leaving the key held.

The usual shape for an input-driven scenario is to `press` through the menu to start
a dive, then `keyDown` a movement key and `step` (or let real time pass) so the
forager moves, then `keyUp` to release it, reading `snapshot()` to see where the
forager ended up.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns an object with at least these fields:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "countdown" | "playing" | "paused" | "cleared" | "gameover",
  depth: <number>,               // the current depth, 1-based
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,
  muted: <boolean>,              // whether the mute toggle is currently on
  planktonRemaining: <number>,   // plankton left in the current maze
  brightness: <number>,          // G, in [0, 1]
  visionRadius: <number>,        // V, the line-of-sight light radius, in px
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}
  windowRadius: <number>,        // R, the outer vision-circle radius, in px
{{/if}}
  sonar: { ready: <boolean>, cooldown: <number>, range: <number> },
                                 // cooldown: seconds until ready; range: E, in tiles, at the current depth
  ink: { ready: <boolean>, cooldown: <number> },

  // The maze grid and its coordinate frame, so a caller can locate tiles and
  // convert between tile (column, row) and logical pixels. These describe the maze
  // this build actually drew — its dimensions and placement are your own design, so
  // report the real values, not any fixed set:
  grid: {
    cols: <number>,    // maze width, in tiles
    rows: <number>,    // maze height, in tiles
    tile: <number>,    // one tile's size, in logical px
    originX: <number>, // logical-px x of column 0's left edge
    originY: <number>, // logical-px y of row 0's top edge
  },

  // The maze, one string per row (top to bottom), one character per column:
  //   '#' wall (rock), '.' open corridor, 'g' the den gate, 'd' the den interior.
  tiles: [ <string>, ... ],      // grid.rows strings, each grid.cols chars

  // The per-tile visibility this instant, same layout as `tiles`:
  //   'u' unrevealed (fog), 'r' remembered (revealed earlier, not lit now), 'l' lit.
  visibility: [ <string>, ... ],

  forager: {
    x: <number>, y: <number>,    // center, in logical px
    tx: <number>, ty: <number>,  // the tile it is on/nearest
    dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",  // its facing — always one of the four
    moving: <boolean>,           // false when it is at rest (not the facing)
  },

  drifters: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, tx: <number>, ty: <number> },
  ],

  predators: [
    {
      kind: "lanternjaw" | "gloamfin" | "flarefish",
      x: <number>, y: <number>, tx: <number>, ty: <number>,
      dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",  // its facing — always one of the four
      state: "den" | "wander" | "chase" | "search",  // 'search' is the Gloamfin's cast-about
      speed: <number>,           // its current speed, in px/s
      alert: <boolean>,          // its detection alert is firing this instant
      lit: <boolean>,            // its body is currently drawn (in light, a sonar mark, a flare, or the alert)
      // Lanternjaw and Flarefish: their light detection range this instant.
      detectRange: <number>,     // R = 128 + 192 * G, in px (omit/null for the Gloamfin)
      // Gloamfin: how far its close-range hearing reaches.
      hearingRange: <number>,    // in px (omit/null for the others)
      // Gloamfin: whether it holds a continuous close-range hearing lock on the
      // forager this instant (the forager is inside its hearing range). While this
      // is true the Gloamfin stays silent, pinging again only once you slip back out
      // of range (specs/predators.md).
      hearingLock: <boolean>,    // (omit/null for the others)
      // Flarefish: its flare state.
      flareCharging: <boolean>,  // the pre-bloom charge-up glow is showing
      flaring: <boolean>,        // the bloom is burning
      flareRadius: <number>,     // the flare's current lit radius, in px (0 when not flaring)
    },
  ],

  // Active sonar wavefronts (the forager's pulse and any Gloamfin pings) in flight.
  pulses: [
    {
      source: "forager" | "gloamfin",
      tint: "cyan" | "violet" | "orange",  // forager cyan; Gloamfin violet, or orange for a "lost you" ping
      ox: <number>, oy: <number>,          // the tile it originated from
      front: <number>,                     // how far the front has traveled, in corridor-steps (tiles)
      range: <number>,                     // its maximum path range, in tiles
    },
  ],

  // Active ink clouds, each fixed where it was released.
  inkClouds: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, radius: <number>, remaining: <number> }, // remaining: seconds left
  ],

  simTime: <number>,   // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

`speed` is the entity's current speed. `alert` is true only during the roughly
`0.5 s` a predator's detection alert is firing. `lit` reflects whether the predator's
body is being drawn this instant (in your light, under a sonar mark, inside a flare,
or during its alert), so it reflects whether a predator is on screen this instant.
`muted` reflects the mute toggle. A predator entry carries the fields relevant to its
kind (the Lanternjaw and Flarefish report `detectRange`; the Gloamfin reports
`hearingRange` and `hearingLock`; the Flarefish reports the flare fields), and may
report the others as `null`.

## The debug overlay

Provide a read-only on-screen overlay showing the game's live internal state, so you
can watch what the simulation is doing while you play. It is toggled with the backtick
key (`` ` ``), off by default, and never changes gameplay; it only draws.

When on, it draws over the running game, legibly, in the game's monospace type, at
least: the current `screen` and `depth`; the `score` and `lives`; the
brightness `G`, the light radius `V`,{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}} the vision-circle radius `R`,{{/if}} and the sonar and ink cooldowns; the plankton remaining; the forager's tile and facing; and, for each predator, its kind, state, tile, speed, and whether its alert is firing. These are the same facts `snapshot()` reports. It is a diagnostic layer rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep it visually plain and clearly separate from the HUD.
